{
  "image_name": "1c16f9433bbacd41cd54d274a1764b89.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates how a cDC1 dendritic cell coordinates “help” from a CD4+ T cell to generate a stronger CD8+ T-cell response. The cDC1 sits in the center and interacts with both T-cell types through antigen presentation: MHC class II engages the CD4+ T-cell receptor on the left, while MHC class I engages the CD8+ T-cell receptor on the right.\n\nThe left side emphasizes helper signaling. The CD4+ T cell provides CD40L to CD40 on the dendritic cell and also releases cytokines such as IL-2 and IL-21. These signals appear to activate or “license” the cDC1. In response, the dendritic cell upregulates key stimulatory molecules and cytokines, including IL-12, CD70, and CD80/CD86.\n\nThe right side shows how these dendritic-cell outputs support the CD8+ T cell. Costimulatory pairs such as CD70–CD27 and CD80/CD86–CD28, together with IL-12 signaling through IL-12R, drive CD8+ T-cell activation. The diagram also suggests that IL-15 and type I interferon contribute to this process, and that the CD8+ T cell increases IL-2Rα, consistent with heightened responsiveness to helper-derived IL-2.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a coordinated three-cell interaction in which CD4 help programs cDC1 to efficiently prime CD8 immunity.",
